# Build-context exclusions for every image built from the REPO ROOT — which is
# all three collectors. services/player-projections builds from its own
# directory and is unaffected by this file (Docker reads .dockerignore from the
# context root, not from the repo root).
#
# This matters more than it looks. Each collector's `workspace-manifests` stage
# does `COPY . .` to reduce the whole tree down to the uv manifests, so
# anything left in the context is transferred on every build. In a real clone
# `.git` alone is tens of megabytes, and a developer with a local `.venv` under
# services/<name>/ would otherwise have it swept into the build.
#
# Kept deliberately narrow: only things no Dockerfile can ever want. Directories
# a future Dockerfile might legitimately copy (contracts/, helm/, tests/) stay
# in the context — they are small, and excluding them buys nothing but a
# confusing "file not found" a year from now.

# Git metadata. A worktree's .git is a 74-byte pointer file; a real clone's is
# the whole object store, and CI builds from a real clone.
**/.git
**/.gitignore

# Python build and test detritus. A host .venv copied into the image would also
# be the wrong platform's wheels.
**/.venv
**/venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.hypothesis
**/.coverage
**/.coverage.*
**/htmlcov

# Agent and editor state
.claude
.idea
.vscode
**/*.swp

# Docs and generated artifacts — no Dockerfile reads these
docs
load-results
load-report.txt
**/*.tgz
